test_polar_datamax
==================

Unit tests for the DataMax subsystem (src/backend/polar_datamax).  The single
SQL entry point

    SELECT test_polar_datamax(readpath text);

runs a sequence of in-backend checks against the datamax control structure,
meta file, WAL removal/archive logic and the WAL parser.  The argument is the
absolute path of the reference WAL image checked in next to this test
(000000010000000000000001).


How it is exercised
-------------------

The whole body is guarded by

    if (!polar_enable_shared_storage_mode)
        PG_RETURN_VOID();

and pg_regress spins up a plain local-filesystem cluster, so `make check` only
proves the module builds and the extension loads -- the body returns
immediately.  The body is run for real by the TAP test
src/test/polar_datamax/t/010_polar_datamax_unit.pl, which stands up a
shared-storage node and executes it as a CI gate (that test is the canonical,
always-in-sync way to run the body; pg_regress just passes the fixture path via
PG_ABS_SRCDIR for the no-op path).


The WAL fixture and its constants
---------------------------------

000000010000000000000001 is a real 16 MB-segment WAL image: at a 16 MB
wal-segsize the file is exactly one whole segment (segment base LSN 0x1000000),
and test_polar_datamax_create_walfile() writes it verbatim into a segment for
polar_datamax_parse_xlog() to walk.  The four constants in
test_polar_datamax_parse_xlog() are derived from that image and MUST be
regenerated together whenever the WAL format changes (an XLOG_PAGE_MAGIC bump --
check `od -An -tx2 -N2 000000010000000000000001` against XLOG_PAGE_MAGIC, PG17 =
0xD116).  A stale fixture makes every WAL page fail validation ("could not find
a valid record after ...").

To regenerate on the current build:

1. initdb a scratch cluster with `--wal-segsize=16` (full_page_writes=off,
   fsync=off, a distinct port, unix_socket_directories='/tmp').  A fresh cluster
   starts in segment 000000010000000000000001, base LSN 0x1000000, already
   several MB in from bootstrap WAL.
2. Generate WAL until it passes the end of segment 1
   (pg_current_wal_lsn() > '0/2000000') with many small inserts
   (insert into t select g, repeat('x',40) from generate_series(1,N) g; in a
   loop); do NOT use pg_switch_wal().  CHECKPOINT.
3. cp $PGDATA/pg_wal/000000010000000000000001 to this directory (copy it before
   a checkpoint can recycle-rename it, or from a preserved copy).
4. With `pg_waldump -p <dir> -s 0/1000000 -e 0/2000000`, pick record-start LSNs
   (each printed `lsn:` is a record start; offset = lsn - 0x1000000,
   page = offset/8192):
     valid_lsn      = end of the segment's first record (= the 2nd record's
                      lsn, e.g. 0x10000A0);
     received_lsn   = a record start near the end of the segment that is
                      same-page-contiguous with its predecessor (both records on
                      the same 8 KB page, so it is the exact EndRecPtr of the
                      preceding record and the case-2 assert
                      received_lsn == valid_lsn holds after parsing the intact
                      image);
     last_valid_lsn = a same-page-contiguous record start a few KB before
                      received_lsn;
     modify_start   = last_valid_lsn - 0x1000000 (its in-segment byte offset).
   Everything else in the body uses wal_segment_size-relative math and adapts to
   16 MB automatically; only these four LSNs are segsize-specific.
